      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-8742",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "If $\\mathbf F$ and $\\mathbf G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $\\nabla \\cdot (k \\mathbf F) = k \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F$\n\n- $\\nabla \\cdot (\\mathbf F + \\mathbf G) = \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F + \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf G$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207\\cdot (F-3G) & =\u2207\\cdot (F)+\u2207\\cdot (-3G) \\\\  & =\u2207\\cdot F-3\u2207\\cdot G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{F}.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207\\cdot F & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}({x}^{2}+y)+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(xy-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2x+x-2y \\\\  & =3x-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207\\cdot (F-3G) & =\u2207\\cdot F-3\u2207\\cdot G \\\\  & =(3x-2y)-3\\cdot (x+y) \\\\  & =-5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073221/example-8742.json",
          "file_sha256": "5b59d0acf741cefcb0721ceacd177108361f53e1e7de814de120faea7fff6e75"
        }
      ],
